WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.017 --> 00:00:01.850
(truck honking)

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.080
- [Reporter] Across the country

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.080
from Kellogg cereal plants in Michigan

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.840
to coal mines in Alabama,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.633
union workers are on strike.

00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:10.173
- We have to do what we have to do.

00:00:10.173 --> 00:00:12.438
This is our lives, this is our livelihood.

00:00:12.438 --> 00:00:14.140
- [Reporter] They're
demanding higher wages,

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.300
better benefits, and
improved working conditions.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.482
- [Leader] When do we want it?

00:00:17.482 --> 00:00:18.330
- [Strikers] Now.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.170
- Speaking to workers and labor leaders

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.140
in different sectors of the economy,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.400
what I really heard

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.910
was that there's a lot of
frustration among workers

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.890
and even distrust of their employers.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.020
And so, you know, these folks

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.800
feel as though it was revealed
to them during the pandemic

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:34.760
that their employers

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.060
aren't necessarily looking
out for their best interests,

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:38.970
and that they need to
advocate for themselves.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.510
And this is what unions are
really hoping to capitalize on.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.800
- [Reporter] Meanwhile,
many non-union workers

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.200
are looking to collective action

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.960
to improve their conditions.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.180
Since the COVID-19 pandemic began,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.590
workers from across a
wide range of industries

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.950
from trucking, to media, to museums,

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.870
to higher ed, to bookstores,

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:58.703
are working to

00:00:58.703 --> 00:01:01.475
or have successfully
unionized their workplaces.

00:01:01.475 --> 00:01:04.475
(strikers chanting)

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.330
So, what's behind this new worker unrest?

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:12.343
Is it just a passing moment or
is this shift likely to last?

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.570
Before the pandemic, labor
unrest was on the rise.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:20.700
In 2018 and 2019 work
stoppages ticked upwards

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.070
with 20 and 25 respectively.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.980
But pre-pandemic American labor

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.760
was not in a position of strength.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.960
For decades now, private
sector union membership numbers

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.600
have been in decline.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.870
- Private sector workers
were really beaten down

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.120
before the pandemic.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.560
The unionization numbers were low

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.770
and they weren't really
striking in great numbers,

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.700
and they weren't really winning

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.780
particularly strong contracts.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.020
- [Reporter] The economic disruption

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.440
brought on by the coronavirus
gave workers new leverage.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.550
Data compiled by Cornell University

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.310
found that the number of
workers on strike in 2021

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.780
increased steadily from
the beginning of the year.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.750
So far, these actions have
produced mixed results.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.370
- Workers at Deere just this week ratified

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.220
a new six year contract,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:05.870
and this brings to an end a strike

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.600
that had been ongoing for over a month.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.960
It involved more than
10,000 workers at Deere,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.640
it was taking place at Deere

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.590
where there hadn't been such
labor activity in 35 years.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.370
And so this just really speaks to the...

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.010
I think the groundswell of frustration

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:22.870
that we're seeing among American workers,

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:26.410
and also the power that
unions feel that they have

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:27.430
at this point in time.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.897
- [Reporter] In a statement the UAW wrote,

00:02:29.897 --> 00:02:32.597
"Our members' willingness
to strike in order to attain

00:02:32.597 --> 00:02:34.097
"a better standard of living

00:02:34.097 --> 00:02:35.987
"in a more secure retirement,

00:02:35.987 --> 00:02:38.657
"resulted in a groundbreaking contract

00:02:38.657 --> 00:02:41.060
"and sets a new standard for workers."

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.020
In a statement to the
"Wall Street Journal",

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.630
Dear Chief Executive, John May

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.817
said, "We're giving
employees the opportunity

00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:48.467
"to earn wages and benefits

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:50.227
"that are the best in our industries.

00:02:50.227 --> 00:02:52.177
"We have faith that, in
return, our employees

00:02:52.177 --> 00:02:53.997
"will find new and better ways

00:02:53.997 --> 00:02:56.240
"to improve our competitiveness."

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.750
- Unions build the middle-class.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.310
You just put power in
the hands of workers.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.630
- [Reporter] Despite an
endorsement from the president,

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:04.830
earlier this year

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.850
a drive to unionize an Amazon
warehouse in Alabama failed

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.080
when workers overwhelmingly
voted against collective action.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.490
Two closely watched
upcoming unionization drives

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:18.260
include one in an Amazon
warehouse in Staten Island

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:22.110
and another at some Starbucks
stores in Buffalo, New York.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.990
- When employers are fighting unions,

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:27.880
they will bombard workers
with text messages

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.770
or in-app messages.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.760
They'll also, during working hours,

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.365
require the employees to
listen to anti-union messages

00:03:36.365 --> 00:03:38.340
about why they shouldn't unionize

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.910
where there's no law requiring equal time.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:42.870
- [Reporter] Workers
interested in creating a union,

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:46.260
form committees dedicated
to advancing unionization.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.670
Oftentimes, these committees
lobby their colleagues

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.230
through personal advocacy,

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.180
but committees also get their message out

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.210
with pro-union literature,
text messages, events,

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.780
and today, social media campaigns.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.740
In November, the company's
CEO, Howard Schultz,

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.100
traveled to Buffalo to
make a personal appeal

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.410
to Starbucks employees.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:05.910
- We're not a perfect company.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.140
We're not perfect people.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:10.463
I'm not a perfect person.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:12.920
Mistakes are made,

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.420
we learn from them,

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.050
and we try and fix them.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:19.160
- I think if the Starbucks
workers in Buffalo

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.370
are able to win their union

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:25.270
in spite of the massive,
massive anti-union onslaught

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:26.760
that they've received,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.230
that will be a promising sign.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.120
- [Reporter] In an open letter
to employees on its website,

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:33.817
a Starbucks executive wrote,

00:04:33.817 --> 00:04:35.157
"Our actions in Buffalo

00:04:35.157 --> 00:04:38.377
"are not about whether we
are pro-union or anti-union.

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:41.497
"It's quite simply that we
are pro-Starbucks partners.

00:04:41.497 --> 00:04:43.327
"As you know, our heritage and culture

00:04:43.327 --> 00:04:44.527
"are built on the belief

00:04:44.527 --> 00:04:47.007
"that by working directly
together as partners,

00:04:47.007 --> 00:04:49.430
"we can build a different
kind of company."

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:52.760
On November 18th, Starbucks
employees in Mesa, Arizona

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.970
said they would like to join a union.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:57.360
So, is this wave of
strikes a tipping point

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.600
for American labor?

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.060
And will we see this unrest
continue to pick up momentum?

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:03.510
- It's too early to tell.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.940
I think there are promising signs.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.940
Right now employers have the
ability to fight off unions,

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.040
whether organizing drives or strikes

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.300
with greater resources
and more legal support.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.210
So, it's very, very difficult

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.870
to organize or to win a strike.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.200
- [Reporter] The current strike wave

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.470
is not large by historical standards,

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:26.850
especially when compared

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.680
with the hundreds that occurred annually

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:30.660
in the 1960s and 70s.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:31.970
Critics of unions warn

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:35.110
that work stoppages and efforts
to influence labor policy

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:38.250
could push up prices for
consumers and slow production.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:41.020
- We live in a different
world than we did in 1950.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.530
There are dozens and
dozens of laws on the books

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.040
that protect employees.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:46.750
There are Safety and Health Rules.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:47.790
Back in the day,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:51.020
when the only place a person
had to go for protection

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.440
was a union contract or a
union grievance procedure,

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.650
today, an employee can pick up the phone

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:00.610
and call the EEOC, or OSHA, or the NLRB.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.950
- [Reporter] But those in
favor of expanded unionization

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:05.000
would dispute that characterization.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.160
- It's a typical anti-union talking point

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:09.240
to say that unions were good for a while

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.730
but we don't need them anymore.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.300
But what we see across the country

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:14.680
is that workers that don't have

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:16.210
the protection of a union contract

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:20.000
can be fired at any time
for almost any reason.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.040
- [Reporter] This is true
of employment at-will.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.350
Often, contract state
that workers can be fired

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:25.330
for good cause,

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:26.163
bad cause,

00:06:26.163 --> 00:06:27.540
or no cause at all.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.990
In recent years, some
states like Michigan,

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.890
Wisconsin, West Virginia, and Kentucky

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:34.800
have passed right-to-work laws.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.390
A right-to-work law gives
workers the freedom to choose

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:40.200
whether or not to join a
labor union in the workplace.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.960
These laws also make it optional

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:44.310
for employees in unionized workplaces

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:47.720
to pay for union dues required
for union representation,

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:49.860
whether they are in the union or not.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.560
Opponents of right-to-work laws

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:53.470
say they allow non union members

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.450
to benefit from union representation

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.330
and union secured benefits

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.370
without sharing in the cost
of negotiating those benefits.

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:02.670
They argue these non-due paying workers

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.910
ultimately lead to union
membership decline.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:08.430
Looking ahead, both Givan and Debruge,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:11.020
said they would be paying
close attention to what happens

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:14.310
with the Protecting the Right
to Organize Act, or PRO Act.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.800
Earlier this year, the
House passed the legislation

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:17.740
which supporters say

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:20.090
would make it easier
for workers to unionize.

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:21.470
Opponents of the law argued

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:23.080
that it would undermine workers' rights

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:24.860
and stifle business.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.720
After the PRO Act was
blocked in the Senate,

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.300
Democrats added some of its elements

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.660
to the Build Back Better Act

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:31.640
or the Budget Reconciliation Bill,

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.704
which they are currently trying
to get through the Congress.

00:07:33.704 --> 00:07:36.287
(gentle music)

